WEBVTT

00:02.394 --> 00:04.652
         ♪<i> It seems today</i>
        <i> that all you see</i> ♪

00:04.730 --> 00:07.731
     ♪<i> Is violence in movies</i>
         <i> and sex on TV</i> ♪

00:07.733 --> 00:11.418
      ♪<i> But where are those</i>
   <i> good old-fashioned values</i> ♪

00:11.495 --> 00:14.421
  ♪<i> On which we used to rely?</i> ♪

00:14.498 --> 00:18.091
 ♪<i> Lucky there's a family guy</i> ♪

00:18.168 --> 00:21.353
    ♪<i> Lucky there's a man who</i>
       <i> positively can do</i> ♪

00:21.430 --> 00:22.913
♪<i> All the things that make us</i> ♪

00:22.915 --> 00:24.189
        ♪<i> Laugh and cry</i> ♪

00:24.266 --> 00:29.987
          ♪<i> He's... a...</i>
       <i> Fam... ily... Guy!</i> ♪

00:37.279 --> 00:38.353
       Well, here we are.

00:38.688 --> 00:40.855
   Oh, and if you hear the guys
      call me "Poopy Thumb,"

00:40.933 --> 00:42.190
   i-it's just a fun nickname,

00:42.268 --> 00:43.767
        it's not something
     that actually happened.

00:43.769 --> 00:45.619
        Hey, Poopy Thumb.

00:45.695 --> 00:48.288
   It was Gulden's Spicy Brown
    Mustard from my sandwich.

00:48.365 --> 00:49.548
          How many times
      do I have to say it?!

00:49.625 --> 00:50.957
        (chuckles): Okay.

00:51.034 --> 00:53.944
      No need to relitigate
   what four of our peers saw.

00:53.946 --> 00:55.387
          Hi, everyone.

00:55.464 --> 00:57.705
   I'm your children's teacher,
           Miss Laura,

00:57.783 --> 01:00.467
  and like pretty much everyone
      in America right now,

01:00.544 --> 01:05.122
          I'm very high
     on legalized edible pot.

01:05.124 --> 01:08.308
    Ha-ha. (inhales sharply)

01:08.385 --> 01:13.313
       It's crazy how much
     your kids look like you.

01:13.390 --> 01:14.722
             Wha....

01:14.800 --> 01:16.483
      Peter, pay attention.

01:16.560 --> 01:18.893
       Sorry, I was having
     a <i> Fast Times </i> daydream

01:18.971 --> 01:20.804
    about that tank top mom.

01:20.806 --> 01:22.247
    Yeah, that's disgusting.

01:22.324 --> 01:24.324
      How would you like it
     if she did that to you?

01:24.401 --> 01:26.543
       ("Moving In Stereo"
       by The Cars plays)

01:36.263 --> 01:37.654
              <i> PETER:</i>
        <i> I chocked on water</i>

01:37.656 --> 01:40.349
  <i> and had to get mouth-to-mouth</i>
           <i> from a guy.</i>

01:42.845 --> 01:44.678
           Five, four,

01:44.754 --> 01:47.755
       three, two, one...

01:47.833 --> 01:49.090
              Yay!

01:49.168 --> 01:50.851
  All right, that's officially

01:50.927 --> 01:53.186
       the latest any of us
        have ever been up.

01:53.188 --> 01:54.780
             -(yawns)
             -Oh-ho!

01:54.857 --> 01:56.506
     A little tired, Stewie?

01:56.508 --> 01:58.766
        No. I just yawned
  because somebody else yawned.

01:58.768 --> 02:00.510
    I could do this all night.
            You tired?

02:00.512 --> 02:02.621
         Oh, no. At home,
    they call me the night...

02:02.623 --> 02:04.748
          (all snoring)

02:07.628 --> 02:09.186
     Is Garrick Utley alive?

02:09.188 --> 02:11.296
           I... (sighs)
       I don't know, Peter.

02:11.373 --> 02:13.357
     Hey, I've been meaning
     to ask you all night--

02:13.359 --> 02:16.543
those vintage pants are amazing,
    where did you get them?

02:16.545 --> 02:19.171
   Oh, they were folded neatly
 on a toilet in the gas station.

02:19.247 --> 02:20.806
          Hey, you know,
   we're starting a text chain

02:20.883 --> 02:22.532
           with some of
     the other young parents.

02:22.534 --> 02:23.475
         Young parents?

02:23.869 --> 02:26.219
    Yeah, new parents like us
     have to stick together.

02:26.296 --> 02:27.813
     (chuckles) Yes, we do.

02:27.815 --> 02:29.389
    Because we're very young.

02:29.466 --> 02:30.874
 Great. Can we get your number?

02:30.876 --> 02:32.726
   Sure. We're Lakeside 4-7...

02:32.802 --> 02:35.303
          He's kidding.
  We'll hit you with the digits.

02:35.381 --> 02:36.880
             Great.

02:36.882 --> 02:39.383
       Oh, my God, Peter,
       did you hear that?

02:39.385 --> 02:41.326
    What? I'm chewing celery.
      I can't hear anything.

02:41.403 --> 02:43.720
 They think Stewie's our first.

02:43.722 --> 02:45.313
   Ah! They think we're young.

02:45.391 --> 02:47.649
       -You're hurting me.
       -This is our chance.

02:47.726 --> 02:49.226
    Our chance to break away

02:49.228 --> 02:51.395
    from the Joes and Bonnies
          of the world.

02:51.397 --> 02:53.488
      Our chance to finally
        make cool friends.

02:53.565 --> 02:54.898
     Well, I don't know what
       you're saying, Lois,

02:54.900 --> 02:57.751
          but that horse
       is the dumbest horse

02:57.827 --> 03:00.012
  I have ever seen in my life.

03:00.088 --> 03:01.588
               <i> MAN:</i>
         <i> It hurt to hear,</i>

03:01.590 --> 03:05.425
          <i> but deep down,</i>
 <i> I respected him for saying it.</i>

03:05.502 --> 03:07.577
         <i> Finally someone</i>
      <i> had told me the truth.</i>

03:07.579 --> 03:11.264
 And that boy grew up to have a
 multiplatinum recording career.

03:11.341 --> 03:13.358
      Hi, I'm Richard Marx,

03:13.435 --> 03:15.843
            and I suck
      at making clay horses,

03:15.921 --> 03:18.755
 but ask your wife if she cares.

03:18.757 --> 03:21.366
       ♪<i> Wherever you go</i> ♪

03:21.443 --> 03:23.702
       ♪<i> Whatever you do</i> ♪

03:23.779 --> 03:26.854
    ♪<i> I will be right here</i> ♪

03:26.932 --> 03:29.666
      ♪<i> Waiting for you.</i> ♪

03:35.699 --> 03:37.290
        (phone vibrates)

03:38.293 --> 03:39.867
       (gasps) Oh, my God,
     it's from Matt and Kate

03:39.945 --> 03:41.053
       on that text chain.
               Uh,

03:41.463 --> 03:45.615
"Heading out for 'ritas downtown
 with the other young parents.

03:45.617 --> 03:47.208
          Meet in 15?"

03:47.210 --> 03:49.228
    Oh, Peter, we have to go!

03:49.304 --> 03:50.604
              Now?

03:55.644 --> 03:56.735
          Peter, look.

03:56.737 --> 03:58.812
         The menu doesn't
        have dollar signs.

03:58.888 --> 04:01.222
       It just has a number
          and a period.

04:01.300 --> 04:03.133
               Ah!
   I feel like I'm in Brooklyn!

04:03.135 --> 04:05.635
    Damn it, all the employees
   are wearing regular clothes,

04:05.637 --> 04:07.746
        so it's impossible
     to ask anybody for help.

04:12.828 --> 04:14.235
              MATT:
            Griffins.

04:15.314 --> 04:17.330
      Uh, you want to sit at
     the high-seat low-table,

04:17.407 --> 04:18.757
   or the low-seat high-table?

04:18.834 --> 04:21.167
      (indistinct chatter)

04:21.169 --> 04:23.095
         How about those
      four old barber chairs

04:23.171 --> 04:25.913
   around the operating tables
  from the state insane asylum?

04:25.991 --> 04:27.507
   Ah, isn't this place great?

04:27.509 --> 04:28.675
            Amazing.

04:28.752 --> 04:30.936
      Hi, my name is Isaac.
      I'll be your server.

04:31.013 --> 04:32.271
        Have you all eaten
         with us before?

04:32.273 --> 04:34.439
          No, but I get
      how restaurants work.

04:34.441 --> 04:36.525
       Now, what can I get
          for you, hon?

04:39.596 --> 04:41.280
        Well, this is us.

04:41.282 --> 04:43.673
          Boy, I almost
  don't want this night to end.

04:43.675 --> 04:45.525
           -Me either.
      -Well, I got an idea.

04:45.602 --> 04:48.120
 You mind if I use your bathroom
        to do number two?

04:48.196 --> 04:51.456
   Wow, your place is amazing.

04:51.533 --> 04:54.851
      A pool, a spa, and all
    the lights are on dimmers.

04:54.853 --> 04:57.129
   <i> And </i> a digital thermostat.

04:57.205 --> 04:59.372
      Oh, my God, it's like
      we're on a spaceship.

04:59.449 --> 05:02.359
   Well, there's an open unit
   if you guys are interested.

05:02.361 --> 05:04.786
         -(toilet flush)
   -Is the toilet a bit better
           in that one?

05:04.863 --> 05:05.804
            Actually,
    it's kind of embarrassing,

05:05.881 --> 05:07.306
          but the toilet
      in that one is shaped

05:07.382 --> 05:08.765
   like a Hungry Hungry Hippo.

05:17.818 --> 05:19.392
      I don't know, Peter.

05:19.469 --> 05:22.062
       It just doesn't feel
    like the responsible thing

05:22.138 --> 05:23.897
    to abandon Chris and Meg.

05:23.973 --> 05:27.067
   The polka dot top goes with
  the white bottoms, too, right?

05:27.143 --> 05:29.219
         Oh, definitely.
       Very old Hollywood.

05:29.221 --> 05:32.222
         Perfect. I mean,
   I guess they are teenagers.

05:32.224 --> 05:35.408
 -And helicopter parents, right?
              -What?

05:35.485 --> 05:37.693
       Helicopter parents.
      It's something I read.

05:37.695 --> 05:39.320
               -Oh.
         -Now you say it.

05:39.398 --> 05:41.414
            -Say what?
       -Helicopter parents.

05:41.491 --> 05:44.343
       -Helicopter parents.
            -Exactly.

05:47.330 --> 05:50.423
 Now, I stocked the freezer with
 plenty to eat while we're gone.

05:50.425 --> 05:53.334
       -On the top shelf...
 <i> -CHRIS: But all I heard was...</i>

05:53.412 --> 05:56.671
            Not pizza.
      Not pizza. Not pizza.

05:56.673 --> 05:59.099
 And finally, this is calamari.

05:59.175 --> 06:01.360
       This is my placenta.
        Are you listening?

06:01.436 --> 06:03.253
    You're gonna want to watch
          where I point.

06:03.255 --> 06:05.922
       -Calamari, placenta.
         -Who died again?

06:05.924 --> 06:09.034
     Meg, listen to the lies.
           Nobody died.

06:09.111 --> 06:12.204
     An aunt of your father's
       or mine is very ill.

06:12.280 --> 06:13.930
        And why is Stewie
         going with you?

06:13.932 --> 06:15.599
   Stewie loved Uncle Grandpa.

06:15.601 --> 06:17.283
           Wait, uncle?
      I thought you said...

06:17.360 --> 06:19.461
       Helicopter parents.
         Let's go, Lois.

06:24.626 --> 06:27.444
    Wow, they're really gone.

06:27.446 --> 06:29.221
    You know what that means.

06:29.297 --> 06:31.556
              BOTH:
   We can finally crate Brian!

06:31.558 --> 06:35.635
     He tricked us, and we're
  still waiting on that lasagna.

06:35.712 --> 06:37.562
   Chris, that was the trick.

06:37.639 --> 06:38.730
               Oh.

06:38.732 --> 06:40.399
         He tricked us!

06:44.963 --> 06:47.147
Ah, look, we're in apartment 4G.

06:47.223 --> 06:48.465
        Like the iPhone.

06:48.467 --> 06:50.634
       That's stupid, Lois.
    Don't ever say that again.

06:50.636 --> 06:53.078
   Hey, how's everything going
            in there?

06:53.155 --> 06:55.730
  Great. We're in apartment 4G,
         like the iPhone.

06:55.807 --> 06:57.323
           (chuckles):
        That's so twisted.

06:57.400 --> 06:59.084
      -Did you make that up?
              -Yes.

06:59.161 --> 07:01.569
      You guys get settled,
  then a few of us are gonna sit

07:01.647 --> 07:04.238
      by the firepit and say
   "mid-century modern" a lot,

07:04.316 --> 07:05.757
      if you want to join.

07:05.759 --> 07:08.093
          -They like us.
       -This is it, Peter.

07:08.170 --> 07:10.336
        We're gonna vape.
        We're gonna vape!

07:10.413 --> 07:13.432
       It's causing deaths,
      but not that many yet.

07:17.663 --> 07:21.589
    Ugh, I can't believe I ate
  both Tupperwares of calamari.

07:21.667 --> 07:23.667
        (belches, groans)

07:23.669 --> 07:24.851
      But I'm still hungry.

07:24.928 --> 07:26.019
  What do we have left to eat?

07:26.021 --> 07:27.504
        I'm not gonna say
        we're out of food,

07:27.506 --> 07:31.283
      but anything still in
 the fridge I have made love to.

07:31.359 --> 07:35.120
   Man, this broom-handle-poked
      tapioca is delicious.

07:35.197 --> 07:37.197
    -Chris had sex with that.
             -(spits)

07:37.273 --> 07:40.292
     -Relax, I didn't finish.
               -Oh.

07:49.361 --> 07:51.528
       All right, Stewie,
       you have a playdate

07:51.530 --> 07:54.547
           with Cobain,
     Siddhartha and Atticus.

07:54.624 --> 07:57.717
      And if anyone asks,
you have plenty of sunscreen on.

07:57.794 --> 08:00.220
         -But I don't...
              -Bye.

08:00.296 --> 08:03.056
         Hey, I'm Cobain.
     Want a strawberry Quik?

08:03.132 --> 08:05.225
    Sure, I want a strawberry,
      but what's the hurry?

08:05.301 --> 08:07.319
       Stewie Griffin, 4G,
         like the iPhone.

08:07.395 --> 08:10.322
         Ha. I like you.
   Our deck needed a wild card.

08:10.398 --> 08:12.382
         Cobain, now that
      you're my best friend,

08:12.384 --> 08:13.733
    can I ask you a question?

08:13.810 --> 08:15.160
        When is nap time?

08:15.237 --> 08:17.662
        Because it's 2:15,
    and I'm about to collapse.

08:17.664 --> 08:18.813
     Oh, we don't take naps.

08:18.815 --> 08:21.315
             No naps?
  Well, then, when is bed time?

08:21.393 --> 08:22.149
            Whenever.

08:22.227 --> 08:23.484
    My God, when do you grow?

08:23.562 --> 08:26.563
       -We don't. I'm six.
           -You're six?

08:26.565 --> 08:29.415
      -Why are you so small?
  -I only eat tofu and seaweed.

08:29.492 --> 08:32.402
       -Nothing in a cage.
   -You've never had parakeet?

08:32.404 --> 08:33.661
               No.
            Have you?

08:33.739 --> 08:36.598
  No, but it looks really yummy
         in the cartoons.

08:39.911 --> 08:42.336
      (indistinct chatter)

08:43.339 --> 08:45.415
   All right, Peter, we can't
   look like we don't belong,

08:45.417 --> 08:48.418
    so when they ask you what
   you want to drink, just say

08:48.420 --> 08:52.922
      -"Local. Micro. Brew."
       -Local. Micro. Brew.

08:52.924 --> 08:54.382
       Local. Micro. Brew.

08:54.459 --> 08:56.034
           Hey, Peter,
   what can I get you to drink?

08:56.111 --> 08:57.518
        (British accent):
  Give us a drop o' sherry, luv.

08:57.596 --> 08:59.596
             -Peter!
        -(regular accent):
     I'm-I'm sorry. I mean...

08:59.598 --> 09:01.665
       Local. Micro. Brew.

09:06.938 --> 09:09.714
 Hi, we're Lois and Peter in 4G.

09:09.791 --> 09:11.457
 I really like your green pants.

09:11.459 --> 09:14.052
     Oh, thanks, I found them
    folded neatly on a toilet.

09:14.129 --> 09:17.055
          I-I mean, I...
 I only buy pants from companies

09:17.132 --> 09:19.707
    that donate the same pair
    to a Third World country.

09:19.785 --> 09:23.136
   Run home with these expired
     military condoms, Ndugu.

09:23.212 --> 09:24.712
Run and make sure they use them.

09:24.790 --> 09:26.982
("Main Title (Golden Ticket/Pure
     Imagination)" playing)

09:33.298 --> 09:35.222
          -(music stops)
            -(grunts)

09:35.300 --> 09:37.409
        (sucking on teeth
       and clicking tongue)

09:54.761 --> 09:56.261
      (bicycle bell rings)

09:57.097 --> 09:58.838
      I found Mom and Dad.

09:58.915 --> 10:00.673
           Wait, what?
         Where are they?

10:00.750 --> 10:02.917
  Well, first I need to explain
        how I found them.

10:02.994 --> 10:04.510
Let me start from the beginning.

10:04.587 --> 10:06.438
      You know how I operate
     a small Internet company

10:06.514 --> 10:08.756
       where I buy and sell
         chlorine, right?

10:08.834 --> 10:11.109
            -No. What?
        -Chris' Chlorine?

10:11.186 --> 10:15.096
        -Chris' Chlorine?
  -Chris, I have no idea what...

10:15.173 --> 10:16.840
        Chris' Chlorine!

10:16.842 --> 10:18.432
     Stop just yelling that!

10:18.510 --> 10:20.693
      Well, anyway, my guy
      at Millennial Village

10:20.770 --> 10:24.347
   Luxury Apartments was buying
    extra chlorine this week.

10:24.349 --> 10:26.458
   Seems they've been finding
   a lot of loose burger meat

10:26.534 --> 10:27.684
          in the pool.

10:27.686 --> 10:29.853
        And who do we know
    that likes to go swimming

10:29.855 --> 10:31.204
     with loose burger meat?

10:31.280 --> 10:34.524
        -Oh, my God, Dad!
             -Bingo.

10:34.526 --> 10:36.468
      But more importantly,
        are you satisfied

10:36.544 --> 10:38.470
            with your
     chlorine supplier, Meg?

10:38.546 --> 10:40.530
       Do you realize that
      most people don't have

10:40.532 --> 10:43.141
     a personal relationship
   with their chlorine dealer?

10:43.218 --> 10:45.143
       How crazy is that?

10:45.220 --> 10:46.552
       Chris, we got to go
    to that apartment building

10:46.629 --> 10:48.388
      -and bust Mom and Dad.
         -(phone ringing)

10:48.464 --> 10:51.149
     I'm on board, Meg, just
   got to take this call first.

10:51.151 --> 10:54.394
    Christopher's Catamarans.
  Been running a side business.

10:54.396 --> 10:55.654
             Hello?

10:55.730 --> 10:56.804
        Aw, wrong number.

10:56.882 --> 10:58.214
   Anyway, Meg, are you happy

10:58.216 --> 11:00.325
  with your catamaran supplier?

11:02.496 --> 11:05.738
   Shouldn't we be confronting
      our parents right now?

11:05.815 --> 11:09.225
     I know, it's so crazy
how they moved out of the house.

11:09.227 --> 11:11.169
           But you know
       what's really crazy?

11:11.246 --> 11:14.488
       Paying big showroom
      prices for catamarans.

11:14.566 --> 11:17.324
  Meg, do you want to pay for
someone else's brick and mortar?

11:17.326 --> 11:19.585
      Or do you want to pay
         for your own fun

11:19.662 --> 11:21.254
    and freedom on the waves?

11:21.330 --> 11:23.497
       -I really think that
    we should be getting back.
         -(phone ringing)

11:23.499 --> 11:26.351
        Hang on, hang on.
     I got to get this call.

11:26.428 --> 11:28.244
             Randy's
    Screen and Window Supply.

11:28.246 --> 11:29.762
            That's not
  another one of my businesses.

11:29.839 --> 11:31.356
   I run an answering service.

11:31.433 --> 11:33.358
      Although I'd be remiss
       if I didn't mention

11:33.435 --> 11:36.753
   that I am dying to get into
 the screen and window business.

11:36.755 --> 11:38.345
     If you have an in, Meg,

11:38.423 --> 11:40.590
  just anyone in the window game
     I could take to coffee,

11:40.592 --> 11:42.926
     you know, and just pick
    their brains about win...

11:42.928 --> 11:45.261
            No, Chris.
       I'm sorry, I don't.

11:45.263 --> 11:47.706
       Yeah, no, it's cool.
      Sorry. I'm still here.

11:47.782 --> 11:50.450
          (phone rings)

11:50.452 --> 11:53.962
  Windows and Screens Unlimited,
        we beat any price.

11:58.626 --> 12:00.460
          So, Griffins,

12:00.462 --> 12:04.055
        what do you think
  of our little oasis in Quahog?

12:04.132 --> 12:05.372
            I like it,
       but I can't believe

12:05.450 --> 12:07.374
    we're all going to college
            next year.

12:07.452 --> 12:08.542
    Peter, that's too young.

12:08.544 --> 12:10.470
     I mean, have you gotten
        a reverse mortgage

12:10.546 --> 12:13.064
    -like Joe Namath suggests?
            -Too old.

12:13.141 --> 12:15.400
              KATE:
        Hey, gang, there's
     a new couple moving in.

12:15.477 --> 12:17.736
       Look at that, Lois.
 We're no longer the new couple.

12:17.738 --> 12:19.320
        The heat is off.

12:22.317 --> 12:24.242
           -Oh, my God.
             -I know.

12:24.319 --> 12:25.493
          That's the guy
  from the catamaran commercial.

12:31.400 --> 12:34.644
         So, you two just
      moved to the complex?

12:34.646 --> 12:37.238
         What did you say
      your names were again?

12:37.315 --> 12:39.482
            I'm Dylan
   and this is my wife, Dylan.

12:39.484 --> 12:42.652
     Oh... same name couple.
        That's the dream.

12:42.654 --> 12:45.079
    I have an idea, why don't
    we all play a board game?

12:45.156 --> 12:46.506
           Great idea.

12:46.658 --> 12:49.917
    Have you guys ever played
 <i> The Wrath of the Star Eclipse:</i>

12:49.995 --> 12:52.270
             <i> Empire:</i>
     <i> Twilight of Destruction?</i>

12:52.272 --> 12:55.164
   Wh-What is that? Is that...
      is that like <i> Sorry!</i> ?

12:55.166 --> 12:58.184
         Um, does <i> Sorry!</i>
       have a 900-sided die

12:58.261 --> 13:00.186
       and its own language
            of rules?

13:00.263 --> 13:01.838
     Oh, we love that game.

13:01.840 --> 13:04.282
   Yeah, that sounds like fun.

13:04.359 --> 13:07.176
  Why don't you guys set it up,
  and we'll get our new friends

13:07.178 --> 13:08.678
     drinks from the kitchen,
               huh?

13:08.680 --> 13:11.289
    What the hell do you guys
       think you're doing?

13:11.366 --> 13:14.275
         I could ask you
 the same question, and I shall.

13:14.277 --> 13:16.202
    What the hell do you guys
       think you're doing?!

13:16.279 --> 13:17.444
          I'll tell you
        what we're doing--

13:17.464 --> 13:20.114
  having fun for the first time
          in our lives.

13:20.191 --> 13:21.541
       And we're not gonna
       let you ruin that.

13:21.617 --> 13:24.694
   Well, we're gonna expose you
    as the old farts you are.

13:24.696 --> 13:26.787
   Oh, you're gonna expose us?

13:26.865 --> 13:27.864
    Did you hear that, Peter?

13:27.866 --> 13:29.732
           Don't care,
      long as you don't pan

13:29.734 --> 13:31.392
    no nuggets off me claim.

13:32.554 --> 13:34.270
        I went way back.

13:38.819 --> 13:40.652
     Okay, Peter, your turn.

13:40.728 --> 13:42.303
              Uh...

13:42.380 --> 13:43.804
       Why are you looking
     at your navigation chart

13:43.882 --> 13:45.139
        when we're still
        at the beginning

13:45.216 --> 13:46.399
    of the first build phase?

13:46.475 --> 13:49.143
    You know what I like to do
        when I play games?

13:49.220 --> 13:51.145
 Ask people when they were born.

13:51.222 --> 13:52.388
       Pete, you go first.

13:52.390 --> 13:54.724
   Oh, that's an easy question.
              19...

13:54.726 --> 13:57.001
          (stammering)

13:57.078 --> 14:01.747
   Uh, uh, nine... 90. 90. 11.

14:01.824 --> 14:03.583
       I was born on 9/11.

14:03.659 --> 14:05.176
   Well, what about you guys?

14:05.253 --> 14:07.403
          You seem like
       a very happy couple,

14:07.405 --> 14:09.496
      yet I haven't seen you
          even kiss yet.

14:09.498 --> 14:12.166
        This is why I said
     we should've practiced.

14:12.243 --> 14:14.018
   I can't kiss at the moment.

14:14.095 --> 14:16.429
 I have raging HPV in my throat.

14:16.505 --> 14:19.506
           Compliments
   of m'lady's Florida sojourn.

14:19.584 --> 14:22.101
      What a fun Gen Z time
          we're having.

14:22.178 --> 14:23.753
     We should take a photo.

14:23.755 --> 14:27.490
Lois, why don't you take a photo
     and AirDrop it to us?

14:29.594 --> 14:32.095
       Of course, AirDrop.

14:32.097 --> 14:33.329
          That's easy.

14:34.690 --> 14:36.282
             (gasps)
           Oh, whoops.

14:36.359 --> 14:38.192
        Dropped my phone.
            (chuckles)

14:38.194 --> 14:40.361
       Peter, can you help
         me pick this up?

14:41.364 --> 14:44.031
    What the hell is AirDrop?
          Is that Wi-Fi?

14:44.109 --> 14:46.217
   -I think it's a medical term
           for a fart.
   -Is that the button you hit

14:46.294 --> 14:47.719
         -on the plane?
-I never turn it off on a plane.

14:47.795 --> 14:50.296
      -How do you ask Siri?
   -I think you mean "celery."

14:50.373 --> 14:52.448
          -Hey, celery.
      -It's definitely Siri.

14:52.450 --> 14:53.799
        -Yeah, I'm wrong.
      -Peter, we got to get
           out of this.

14:53.876 --> 14:55.542
     I'll-I'll just throw up,
   everyone will have to leave.

14:55.620 --> 14:58.062
     -Can you really do that?
      -Yeah, I taught myself
         in sixth grade.

14:58.139 --> 15:00.356
        -I'm gonna do it.
   -Okay. Okay. I'm in. I'm in.

15:02.718 --> 15:05.128
            (retches)

15:05.130 --> 15:06.571
          Aw, gee whiz.

15:06.648 --> 15:09.298
         Aw, sorry, guys.
   Looks like the party's over.

15:09.300 --> 15:11.367
   We'll AirDrop another time.

15:16.658 --> 15:18.141
           Ah, Sunday.

15:18.143 --> 15:20.326
      Perfect day to dangle
       my feet in the pool

15:20.328 --> 15:22.203
  with the other heavier wives.

15:23.314 --> 15:26.633
      Ah, loose burger meat
         on a sunny day.

15:27.761 --> 15:29.743
This is nice, isn't it, Beverly?

15:29.821 --> 15:31.337
         Bev? Isn't it?

15:31.414 --> 15:33.264
             -Uh-huh.
       -Well, then say it.

15:33.341 --> 15:36.267
     Uh, you guys, the Dylans
     just had the best idea.

15:36.269 --> 15:38.919
      What do you say we all
    go to Quachella with a Q,

15:38.997 --> 15:41.105
        Quahog's three-day
         music festival?

15:41.182 --> 15:43.257
    Oh, music festival, huh?

15:43.334 --> 15:45.109
   You think the von Trapps'll
           stick around

15:45.186 --> 15:46.593
      to pick up their award
            this time?

15:46.595 --> 15:49.338
    I don't know about them,
but they're gonna have holograms

15:49.340 --> 15:52.191
of Tupac and also Nipsey Hussle,
     who I'd never heard of

15:52.268 --> 15:54.343
        and then was told
     to care immensely about.

15:54.345 --> 15:56.287
 So, this was the Dylans' idea?

15:56.364 --> 15:58.197
         Oh, oh, we can't
       take credit for it.

15:58.199 --> 15:59.532
         Any young person
     would've thought of it.

15:59.608 --> 16:02.201
    Do I have to wear a tuxedo
       like Frank and Sammy

16:02.278 --> 16:04.520
         or is that just
     for the people on stage?

16:04.522 --> 16:06.464
               ♪

16:09.285 --> 16:11.636
     Hey, Lois, will you snap
         a 'gram for us?

16:13.473 --> 16:15.531
        So, the-the button
          in the middle?

16:15.533 --> 16:18.217
       Yeah, portrait mode.
     Slap on a Hudson filter.

16:18.294 --> 16:20.369
       The-the big button?
             Peter?!

16:20.371 --> 16:23.464
    Lois, please, I'm doing my
  <i> Charles in Charge </i> word find.

16:23.541 --> 16:26.526
       Now, where are those
       two Ds for "Buddy"?

16:34.477 --> 16:36.285
               ♪

16:37.146 --> 16:39.889
      They-they just left?

16:39.891 --> 16:41.648
      Huh. Thought you kids
        didn't take naps.

16:41.726 --> 16:43.893
   Now you're all just sleeping
    at the bottom of the pool?

16:43.895 --> 16:45.712
           Oh, my God!

16:52.237 --> 16:54.512
         (quiet chatter)

16:54.514 --> 16:55.921
        Guys, we're here.

16:55.998 --> 16:59.166
 Yup, there's the local mosquito
       and gnat population,

16:59.244 --> 17:01.019
 anxiously awaiting my arrival.

17:01.095 --> 17:03.229
            (buzzing)

17:04.599 --> 17:07.674
       All right, time for
     a fallen-arch, flip-flop

17:07.752 --> 17:09.527
   two-mile walk to the gate.

17:21.933 --> 17:23.541
       Uhp, I got a leaf.

17:36.890 --> 17:38.431
             (sighs)

17:41.210 --> 17:43.352
            -(groans)
         -(wind whistles)

17:44.731 --> 17:46.230
             (sighs)

17:51.646 --> 17:53.988
     Geez, we've been walking
         for 20 minutes.

17:55.075 --> 17:57.075
 You guys can tap out any time.

17:57.151 --> 18:00.152
       I know you both have
    searing IPA sun headaches.

18:00.229 --> 18:02.305
        There's ibuprofen
         in my backpack.

18:02.307 --> 18:05.416
        Admit you're old,
         and it's yours.

18:05.493 --> 18:07.159
               No.
              Never.

18:07.161 --> 18:09.420
      We're getting close.
      I can see the stages.

18:09.422 --> 18:12.340
    Oh, there's nothing there.
      That's just a mirage.

18:13.409 --> 18:15.343
        (faint cheering)

18:17.764 --> 18:20.306
        The porta potties
        are a mirage, too.

18:33.004 --> 18:35.354
    Thanks for waiting, guys.
  I really had to go number one.

18:35.431 --> 18:37.506
 Oh, my God, what are we doing?

18:37.508 --> 18:39.117
       Peter just crapped
       in the parking lot.

18:39.193 --> 18:41.510
        -We can't do this.
          -What's wrong?

18:41.512 --> 18:42.620
  Are you guys not super amped?

18:42.622 --> 18:44.771
               No!
          No, we're old!

18:44.849 --> 18:46.198
      We hate this (bleep)!

18:46.200 --> 18:49.535
   -W-Well, this is a surprise.
          -Oh, shut up.

18:49.537 --> 18:52.204
   And they're only teenagers,
        and not cool ones.

18:52.281 --> 18:53.856
        If you two idiots
         weren't wearing

18:53.858 --> 18:57.426
  terrible Warby Parker glasses,
    you would have seen that.

18:58.546 --> 18:59.470
           -Oh, my God!
            -(groans)

18:59.547 --> 19:01.973
        Look, we're sorry
         we lied to you.

19:02.049 --> 19:05.050
   We thought it would be fun
to act like young parents again.

19:05.127 --> 19:07.461
     But you don't have kids
           to have fun.

19:07.463 --> 19:09.647
          You have kids
       to use as an excuse

19:09.724 --> 19:11.131
    to never leave the house.

19:11.209 --> 19:13.634
   Can you guys at least Venmo
       us for the tickets?

19:13.711 --> 19:16.562
   I don't know what that is!

19:16.639 --> 19:19.064
         And that's okay.
               Ha.

19:19.141 --> 19:20.566
  Chris, give us the ibuprofen

19:20.643 --> 19:22.885
       and text the driver
       to pick us up here.

19:22.887 --> 19:25.388
   I don't know how to do it.

19:25.390 --> 19:27.390
           Are we done?
            Thank God.

19:27.392 --> 19:30.209
     I'm gonna go get a water
   at that 7-Eleven over there.

19:31.913 --> 19:33.337
       (entry bell chimes)

19:41.906 --> 19:43.764
     Ugh, I ruined my pants.

19:47.745 --> 19:49.312
         Circle of life.

19:50.248 --> 19:51.397
       (entry bell chimes)

19:55.269 --> 19:57.195
  I hated every minute of this.

20:02.702 --> 20:06.111
Well, we had quite an adventure,
     but I think we learned

20:06.188 --> 20:08.689
        a valuable lesson.
     Isn't that right, Chris?

20:08.766 --> 20:10.449
          I don't know.
    I'd love to stay and talk,

20:10.451 --> 20:13.377
 but I've got a catamaran client
        I got to check on.

20:15.548 --> 20:16.422
             (gasps)

20:17.458 --> 20:20.051
      -Uh, what's going on?
          -Sorry, Chris.

20:20.127 --> 20:22.628
   There's a hungry new player
      in the catamaran game.

20:22.705 --> 20:26.465
  While you were at Quachella,
  I was building relationships.

20:26.542 --> 20:28.801
     That's all the catamaran
       business is, Chris--

20:28.877 --> 20:30.970
         relationships.

20:31.046 --> 20:34.623
             <i> CHRIS:</i>
<i>Losing my best client like that</i>
  <i> was a real epiphany for me,</i>

20:34.625 --> 20:38.218
     and I knew immediately
     that I had two choices:

20:38.220 --> 20:42.055
    I could give up or I could
     sneak into a Ramada Inn

20:42.133 --> 20:44.149
          and wander up
     to an unguarded podium.

20:44.226 --> 20:46.151
       I chose the latter.

20:46.228 --> 20:49.655
         And it has made
       all the difference!

20:49.732 --> 20:52.333
          ("Glory Days"
   by Bruce Springsteen plays)

20:53.586 --> 20:54.752
            ♪<i> Yeah</i> ♪

20:54.829 --> 20:57.496
          ♪ <i> Come on </i> ♪

20:57.573 --> 20:58.464
            ♪<i> Whoo!</i> ♪